WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.763
File validation is about reducing
ambiguity.

2
00:00:02.843 --> 00:00:06.905
Do not trust the Content-Type header by
itself because clients can control or spoof

3
00:00:06.985 --> 00:00:07.159
it.

4
00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:10.837
Do not trust a file extension by itself
because extensions can be missing,

5
00:00:10.917 --> 00:00:13.586
misleading, doubled, or chosen to bypass a
weak check.

6
00:00:13.666 --> 00:00:16.916
A filename ending in .jpg does not prove
the content is a safe image,

7
00:00:16.996 --> 00:00:20.884
and a browser-provided MIME type does not
prove the content is what the business

8
00:00:20.964 --> 00:00:21.428
expects.

9
00:00:21.508 --> 00:00:25.570
Use allowlists for the extensions and file
types required by the specific business

10
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.172
function.

11
00:00:26.252 --> 00:00:29.676
Then validate using multiple signals where
appropriate: extension,

12
00:00:29.756 --> 00:00:33.179
declared type, file signature, parser
behavior, size expectations,

13
00:00:33.259 --> 00:00:34.594
and content expectations.

14
00:00:34.674 --> 00:00:36.937
The depth of inspection should match the
risk.

15
00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:40.499
A profile image workflow might verify
image structure and dimensions.

16
00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:44.815
A document upload workflow might require
stricter scanning and processing controls.

17
00:00:44.895 --> 00:00:48.783
Validation reduces risk, but it should not
become an excuse to accept every file

18
00:00:48.863 --> 00:00:49.153
type.

19
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.554
Original filenames from MultipartFile,
Part,

20
00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.477
or similar APIs are untrusted
client-supplied metadata.

21
00:00:54.557 --> 00:00:57.923
They may contain confusing Unicode
characters, double extensions,

22
00:00:58.003 --> 00:01:00.556
reserved names, path separators, very long
values,

23
00:01:00.636 --> 00:01:04.814
hidden-file patterns, control-like
characters, or platform-specific surprises.

24
00:01:04.894 --> 00:01:08.318
Do not use the original name as a
filesystem path or object storage key.

25
00:01:08.398 --> 00:01:10.893
Generate a server-side storage name or
object key,

26
00:01:10.973 --> 00:01:14.803
and store the original filename only as
metadata when the application needs to

27
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:15.463
display it.

28
00:01:15.543 --> 00:01:17.400
Displayed filenames still need care.

29
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.439
Set length limits, restrict characters
where appropriate,

30
00:01:20.519 --> 00:01:24.001
and escape or encode filenames before
placing them in HTML, JSON, logs,

31
00:01:24.081 --> 00:01:25.880
headers, templates, or audit views.

32
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.035
Preserve user-friendly names only as
untrusted display text.

33
00:01:29.115 --> 00:01:33.003
The safest pattern is to separate
user-facing metadata from storage identity:

34
00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:35.056
the user may see a cleaned original name,

35
00:01:35.136 --> 00:01:39.140
while the system stores and retrieves the
object through a generated identifier.
